    "body": "Background sync requests blocks up to and including the snapshot block from several peers at once, so the snapshot block can be stored before one of its ancestors. `ReceivedBlockTransactions()` then keeps it in `m_blocks_unlinked`, but that isn't persisted. On restart `LoadBlockIndex()` gives it the hardcoded `m_chain_tx_count`, `PopulateBlockIndexCandidates()` makes it a background candidate, and `FindMostWorkChain()` walks back to its parent, which has no chain tx count:\n\n```\nAssertion failed: (pindexTest->HaveNumChainTxs() || pindexTest->nHeight == 0), function FindMostWorkChain, file validation.cpp, line 3217.\n```\n\nIt needs a restart in that window, so it should be rare, but after that the node aborts on every start. The only ways out I found are `-reindex-chainstate` or `-reindex`, and both delete the snapshot chainstate.\n\nFix this by adding the snapshot block to `m_blocks_unlinked` on startup if an ancestor is missing, and not making it a background candidate until then. Both are needed, without the first one background validation stalls. A node that is already stuck starts with the fix and finishes background validation without a reindex.\n\nThe new step in `feature_assumeutxo.py` restarts the node after the snapshot block is submitted out of order, and fails on master. I also reproduced it over p2p with a test peer that withholds the snapshot block's parent.\n\nThese are the two changes from #35307 (closed, the motivation there was `submitblock` before `loadtxoutset`) without its blockfile and VerifyDB changes. cyb3ralbert hit the same assertion while testing #36452 (https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36452#issuecomment-6464524112), which is still needed when the snapshot tip is at the snapshot block. #36149 drops the `BLOCK_HAVE_DATA` check in the branch right below, so whichever is merged second should match it.",
